<Docs
  title="Integrated AI agent"
  description="An agent pane that opens beside your tasks, so you can hand over work without leaving the list you are looking at."
  toc={toc}
>
  <h2 id="pane">The pane</h2>
  <p>
    The agent is a pane in the app, not a separate window or a chat site. It sits next to the list you are
    already in, which means the context you have built up — the task, its project, the tags around it — is
    context the request already has.
  </p>

  <h2 id="delegation">Delegating work</h2>
  <p>
    The pane is for delegation rather than conversation. Requests that read naturally against your own
    lists are the ones it is built for:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>"Group today's triage by what should happen next."</li>
    <li>"Set up the release checklist for next month."</li>
    <li>"Take this task through the coding workflow."</li>
    <li>"Turn these notes into tasks in the right project."</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Because it acts on the same database the rest of the app is reading, the result shows up as real tasks
    you can edit — not as a summary you have to transcribe by hand.
  </p>

  <h2 id="stack">What it inherits</h2>
  <p>
    The pane drives the same agent as the terminal tool, over the Agent Client Protocol, so it inherits that
    configuration rather than reimplementing it:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>The <strong>provider and model</strong> you selected, including fallback when a provider fails before producing output.</li>
    <li>The <strong>tools</strong> the agent has — files, search, shell, and anything you have added as an MCP server.</li>
    <li><strong>Hooks</strong> bound to agent events, so automation outside the app still fires.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The protocol and the client that speaks it are covered on the contributor side in
    <a href="/contributor/integrations#acp">the agent protocol</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="configure">Configuring it</h2>
  <p>
    Nothing needs configuring to use the local lists. To use the agent, pick a provider and give it a key
    — that is one JSON file, documented in <a href="/configuration#agent">Configuration</a>. Tool servers
    and hooks live in the same file.
  </p>

  <Callout tone="note" title="Workflows are the interesting part">
    A single request is useful; a task that carries its own process is better. See
    <a href="/workflows">Semi-automated workflows</a> for the staged version of this.
  </Callout>
</Docs>
